WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.637
Numbers from input should be parsed with functions that let the program distinguish success from failure.

2
00:00:06.637 --> 00:00:12.030
std::from_chars is useful because it is explicit, locale-independent, and reports where parsing stopped.

3
00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:21.571
Functions such as stoi and stol can be appropriate when exceptions are handled deliberately, but they should not be treated as magic validation.

4
00:00:21.571 --> 00:00:28.623
Stream extraction has its own pitfalls because failed parsing, partial parsing, and leftover input must be checked.

5
00:00:28.623 --> 00:00:31.942
Conversion is only the first step.

6
00:00:31.942 --> 00:00:35.675
The converted value must be valid for its purpose.

7
00:00:35.675 --> 00:00:43.972
A port number, timeout, count, array index, file offset, allocation size, enum value, and percentage all have different allowed ranges.

8
00:00:43.972 --> 00:00:47.291
Signed and unsigned conversions deserve special attention.

9
00:00:47.291 --> 00:00:53.098
A negative value converted to an unsigned type can become a very large number.

10
00:00:53.098 --> 00:00:58.076
A value that fits in one type may not fit in another.

11
00:00:58.076 --> 00:01:03.884
Narrowing conversions can lose information or change meaning if they are not checked first.

12
00:01:03.884 --> 00:01:07.617
Integer overflow matters before allocation, indexing, and pointer movement.

13
00:01:07.617 --> 00:01:15.084
A count multiplied by an element size can wrap and produce a smaller allocation than later code expects.

14
00:01:15.084 --> 00:01:20.892
An offset plus a length can wrap and appear to be within a buffer.

15
00:01:20.892 --> 00:01:25.040
Validate counts, sizes, indexes, offsets, and allocation inputs before use.

16
00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:31.677
Ask how the value will be used downstream, not only whether it was syntactically a number.

17
00:01:31.677 --> 00:01:36.655
Secure numeric handling creates typed, range-checked values that later code can trust.
